  "verdict": {
    "kind": "move-after",
    "label": "Ready after 3 changes",
    "title": "You can move django-render to Cloud Run after 3 changes",
    "reason": "The worker, the timers and the files on disk need a change each before Cloud Run; the rest moves as it is."
  },
  "findings": [
    {
      "i": 1,
      "id": "queue-worker",
      "kind": "breaks",
      "title": "A worker waits on a Redis queue",
      "at": "books/celery.py:8",
      "code": "app = Celery(\"books\", broker=os.environ[\"CELERY_BROKER_URL\"])",
      "why": "Cloud Run gives an instance CPU only while it answers a request, so a worker waiting on a queue gets no CPU between requests and its jobs stall. With CPU always allocated it runs, and bills every second whether there is work or not.",
      "fix": "Cloud Tasks: each task becomes a POST to /tasks/<name> on the service, created where the code called .delay() or .enqueue(); a handler does the work and answers 2xx. Retries and rate limits move to the queue.",
      "part": "worker",
      "kb": "background-workers-with-cloud-tasks",
      "subject": "the worker",
      "task": 2
    },
    {
      "i": 2,
      "id": "in-process-timer",
      "kind": "breaks",
      "title": "Timers inside the process",
      "at": "books/celery.py:14",
      "code": "\"schedule\": crontab(hour=2, minute=0),",
      "why": "A service at zero has no process to run a timer, a running one gets no CPU between requests, and with several instances the timer fires once per instance: late, never, or twice.",
      "fix": "Cloud Scheduler on the same cron lines: short work calls POST /cron/<name> on the service with an OIDC token, long work starts a Cloud Run job. Set each job's time zone; the timer ran on the server's, usually UTC.",
      "part": "worker",
      "kb": "cron-and-timers-with-cloud-scheduler",
      "subject": "the timers",
      "task": 3
    },
    {
      "i": 3,
      "id": "local-disk-write",
      "kind": "breaks",
      "title": "Files written to local disk",
      "at": "exports/tasks.py:13",
      "code": "with open(path, \"w\", newline=\"\") as f:",
      "why": "An instance's disk lives in its memory: a file counts against the memory limit, is gone when the instance stops, and other instances never see it, so an upload saved on one is a 404 on the next.",
      "fix": "Write to a Cloud Storage bucket (django-storages' Google Cloud backend for FileField) and hand out signed URLs.",
      "part": "media",
      "kb": "files-and-uploads-with-cloud-storage",
      "subject": "the files on disk",
      "task": 4
    },
    {
      "i": 4,
      "id": "release-migrations",
      "kind": "note",
      "title": "Migrations run in a release step",
      "at": "render.yaml:8",
      "code": "preDeployCommand: python manage.py migrate",
      "why": "Cloud Run has no release phase, and a migration in the container's start command runs once per instance, several at the same time.",
